## Detailed Verification Log

### Step 1 – Reduction to the triangle \(T_k\)

**Claim.**  
Any configuration of \(n\) distinct lines with exactly \(k\) sunny lines can be transformed, without loss of coverage, into a configuration where the \(n-k\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines \(x=1,\dots ,x=n-k\).  

**Verification.**  
A non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\(y=\text{const}\)) or of slope \(-1\) (\(x+y=\text{const}\)).  
*For a fixed column \(x=a\):* a horizontal line meets the column at the single point \((a,\text{const})\); a line of slope \(-1\) meets the column at the single point \((a,c-a)\). Thus each non‑sunny line can cover **at most one** point in any given column.  
Replacing such a line by a vertical line \(x=b\) that passes through a column not yet occupied by a vertical line covers **all** points of that column, certainly covering the point previously covered by the replaced line and possibly more.  
Choosing distinct columns for the replacements yields \(n-k\) distinct vertical lines \(x=1,\dots ,x=n-k\). All points with abscissa \(\le n-k\) are now covered, and the set of uncovered points is exactly  

\[
T_k=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid x+y\le k+1\},
\]

obtained by translating the original condition \(a+b\le n+1\) by the shift \(a\mapsto a-(n-k)\).  

**Conclusion.** The reduction is correct; no gap is present.

---

### Step 2 – A bound for a sunny line inside \(T_k\)

**Claim.**  
For any sunny line, \(|\text{line}\cap T_k|\le\bigl\lfloor (k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\).  

**Argument given.**  
The line is written as \(p x-q y=c\) with \(\gcd(p,q)=1,\;q>0,\;p\neq0,\;p\neq -q\).  
All integer points on the line are \((x_0+qt,\;y_0+pt)\) (\(t\in\mathbb Z\)), so the sum \(x+y\) equals \((x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t\).  
Since the line is sunny, \(p+q\neq0\); therefore the sum changes by at least \(|p+q|\ge1\) when \(t\) changes by one.  
Inside \(T_k\) we have \(2\le x+y\le k+1\). Hence the number of admissible integers \(t\) is at most  

\[
\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1-2}{|p+q|}\Bigr\rfloor+1
   =\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k-1}{|p+q|}\Bigr\rfloor+1.
\tag{*}
\]

If \(|p+q|\ge2\) then \((*)\le\bigl\lfloor(k-1)/2\bigr\rfloor+1=\bigl\lfloor(k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\).  

For \(|p+q|=1\) the solution continues: “the number of admissible \(t\) is at most \(\bigl\lfloor k/q\bigr\rfloor+1\le\bigl\lfloor (k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\).”

**Problem.**  
The inequality \(\bigl\lfloor k/q\bigr\rfloor+1\le\bigl\lfloor (k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\) is **false** when \(k\) is even and \(q=2\).  
Example: \(k=4,\;q=2\) gives \(\bigl\lfloor4/2\bigr\rfloor+1=3\) while \(\bigl\lfloor5/2\bigr\rfloor=2\).  

The correct bound for \(|p+q|=1\) must combine the sum‑restriction \((*)\) with the column‑restriction \(|x|\le k\). The number of admissible \(t\) is actually at most \(\bigl\lfloor (k-1)/q\bigr\rfloor+1\), which **does** satisfy \(\le\bigl\lfloor (k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\) for all \(q\ge2\). This refinement is omitted.

**Classification.** This is a **Justification Gap**: the bound (2.5) is true, but the proof for the case \(|p+q|=1\) is incomplete and contains an incorrect inequality.

---

### Step 3 – Counting argument

**Claim.**  
If the configuration contains exactly \(k\) sunny lines, then  

\[
k\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1}{2}\Bigr\rfloor\ge\frac{k(k+1)}2 .
